<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Cycle racing in the 1960s: Anquetil against Poulidor, Fredborg on the track, Merckx’s arrival, lighter steel bicycles and the difficult beginnings of doping control."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/periods/1960-1969/anquetil-poulidor-paris-1964.webp"/><title>When the racing bicycle found its modern form · 1960–1969 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="periods" danishPath="/perioder/1960-1969/"/><main><article class="wildwest-page decade1960-page">
<header class="wildwest-hero decade1940-hero"><div><p class="section-label">The period 1960–1969</p><h1>When the racing bicycle found<br/><em>its modern form</em></h1></div><p>Two chainrings, five sprockets, friction levers and a light steel frame. The basic form was established. Around the bicycle, the sport became more visible — and its problems harder to hide.</p></header>
<figure class="decade1970-hero-image"><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1960-1969/anquetil-poulidor-paris-1964.webp`} alt="Jacques Anquetil and Raymond Poulidor surrounded by people after the 1964 Tour de France"/><figcaption>Jacques Anquetil on the left and Raymond Poulidor after the 1964 Tour de France. Anquetil won by 55 seconds. Photograph: Anefo / Nationaal Archief, CC0.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-lead decade1940-lead"><p class="section-label">The familiar silhouette</p><p>Remove the jersey, support car and newspaper names, and a good racing bicycle from 1969 already resembles what many would call classically modern: diamond frame, parallelogram derailleur, two chainrings and rim brakes.</p></section>
<section class="era-facts decade1970-facts" aria-label="Highlights from cycle racing in the 1960s">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">Puy de Dôme · 1964</p><h2>Shoulder to shoulder</h2></div><div><p>Jacques Anquetil and Raymond Poulidor divided France in a way that was not merely about results. Anquetil was cool, precise and outstanding against the clock. Poulidor appeared more approachable and kept coming back, although he never wore the Tour’s yellow jersey.</p><p>On Puy de Dôme, they rode close beside each other for a long time. Julio Jiménez won the stage farther up the road. Behind him, Poulidor only opened a gap to Anquetil in the final kilometre and gained about 40 seconds. Anquetil retained the lead by just 14 seconds.</p><p>The final time trial to Paris suited him better. He won the Tour for a fifth time, 55 seconds ahead of Poulidor. The close mountain image remained the race’s defining scene, although the decision came against the clock.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1970-mechanics"><div><p class="section-label">The mechanism settles</p><h2>Ten gears and a hand on the down tube</h2><p>The parallelogram derailleur was not new, but in the 1960s it became the natural solution on the best racing bicycles. Campagnolo’s Record and, from 1967, Nuovo Record became reference points. The rider still moved a friction lever on the down tube and listened for whether the chain was running cleanly.</p><p>Two chainrings at the front and five sprockets at the rear normally gave ten combinations. Six sprockets became more common later. There were no indexed clicks, but the principle of guiding a chain sideways between several sprockets was the same one that would dominate for decades.</p></div><aside aria-label="Typical details on a fine 1960s racing bicycle"><span>Steel frame</span><span>2 × 5 gears</span><span>Friction levers</span><span>Tubular tyres</span><span>Toe clips</span><span>Rim brakes</span></aside></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy dark"><div><p class="section-label">Thin tubes and polished aluminium</p><h2>Lighter without changing material</h2></div><div><p>Steel remained the frame’s basic material. The difference lay in the alloy, butted tubes and the framebuilder’s work. Reynolds 531 had been introduced as early as 1935, but the manufacturer’s own history highlights both Anquetil’s 1964 Tour bicycle and Merckx’s 1969 bicycle as being built with 531.</p><p>Aluminium was used for rims, chainsets, brakes, handlebars and stems. MAFAC’s centre-pull brakes were widespread, while Campagnolo’s brakes used side-pull operation. The two types should not be confused, although both belong on bicycles from the period.</p><p>Holes drilled in components to save grams existed, but “drillium” is particularly associated with the pursuit of low weight that became more visible around the transition to the 1970s. The gain could be measured in grams. A weakened component could be measured in a less pleasant way.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1940-return"><div><span>1967</span><h2>Fredborg’s first world title</h2><p>Niels Fredborg won the 1,000-metre time trial at the track world championships in Amsterdam. He repeated the victory in 1968 and took Olympic silver in Mexico City that same year, just 0.7 seconds behind Pierre Trentin.</p></div><div><span>1969</span><h2>Merckx takes everything</h2><p>Eddy Merckx won his first Tour by 17 minutes and 54 seconds. He also won the points, mountains and combination classifications. The polka-dot mountains jersey did not yet exist; it arrived in 1975.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy rust"><div><p class="section-label">Food between habit and measurement</p><h2>Nutrition did not become modern in a single summer</h2></div><div><p>Large breakfasts, meat, eggs, rice, pasta and sugary foods did not disappear with the 1950s. Riders still carried food in their jersey pockets and received supplies from team staff along the way. Tea, coffee, fruit juice and water were mixed according to the habits of the team and rider.</p><p>At the same time, physiology and more systematic training moved closer to the sport. This does not mean that the entire peloton suddenly followed identical diet plans. Jacques Anquetil’s reputation for champagne and rich meals became part of his legacy, but individual colourful menus should not be treated as a recipe for an entire decade’s nutrition.</p><p>The certain shift was gradual: the body increasingly became something people tried to measure and plan around, rather than merely something replenished through experience and appetite.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">1966–1968</p><h2>Testing came before the turning point</h2></div><div><p>Doping was not concealed in the same way as it would be later. Amphetamines and other stimulants formed part of a culture in which riders, helpers and doctors did not necessarily draw the same line between treatment and performance enhancement.</p><p>The Tour de France carried out doping controls as early as 1966. Riders responded with a short protest against what they saw as interference in their working conditions. It is therefore too simple to say that testing only began after Tom Simpson’s death.</p><p>His death on Mont Ventoux on 13 July 1967 nevertheless made the problem visible to many more people. Simpson collapsed during stage 13 and could not be revived. Tests found amphetamine and alcohol; heat, exhaustion and dehydration were also part of the sequence.</p><p>In 1968, both the Tour and the Olympic Games conducted more formalised testing. The system remained far removed from today’s, but the sport could no longer treat drugs as a purely internal matter.</p></div></section>
<section class="period-feature decade1970-related"><div><p class="section-label">Stories that should be told</p><h2>Five paths through the decade</h2><p>The period provides the overview. The stories continue with Puy de Dôme, the 1966 doping protest, Tom Simpson, Niels Fredborg and Merckx’s first Tour.</p></div><div class="period-feature-links"><a href={`${base}/en/stories/`}><span>Stories</span><strong>From a mountain duel to track cycling</strong><b>See the published stories →</b></a></div></section>
